     In some network topologies it is desirable to perform NAT on traffic
     leaving through the VPN tunnel.  In order to achieve that, the _s_r_c argu‐
     ment is used to negotiate the desired network ID with the peer and the
     _s_r_c_n_a_t parameter defines the true local subnet, so that a correct SA can
     be installed on the local side.

     For example, if the local subnet is 192.168.1.0/24 and all the traffic
     for a specific VPN peer should appear as coming from 10.10.10.1, the fol‐
     lowing configuration is used:

           ike esp from 10.10.10.1 (192.168.1.0/24) to 192.168.2.0/24 \
                   peer 10.10.20.1

     Naturally, a relevant NAT rule is required in pf.conf(5).  For the exam‐
     ple above, this would be:

           match out on enc0 from 192.168.1.0/24 to 192.168.2.0/24 \
                   nat-to 10.10.10.1

     From the peer's point of view, the local end of the VPN tunnel is de‐
     clared to be 10.10.10.1 and all the traffic arrives with that source ad‐
     dress.

